            ("When shares are issued with calls payable in installments, the installment requested immediately upon submitting the application is the:", ["Application money", "Allotment money", "First call money", "Final call money"], 0, "Investors remit application money with their forms; successful applicants then pay allotment money.", "Company Accounts"),
            ("Shares that are forfeited by a company due to the non-payment of call monies by a subscriber may be:", ["Reissued to other investors, often at a discount not exceeding the amount already received", "Destroyed permanently without record", "Converted to sovereign debt", "Divided among directors"], 0, "Forfeited shares can be reissued; any forfeiture profit is credited to Capital Reserve.", "Share Capital"),
            ("What is the primary difference between provisions and general reserves in corporate accounting?", ["Provisions are charges against profit to meet known liabilities of uncertain amount; reserves are appropriations of undistributed profit", "Provisions are cash in bank; reserves are debts", "Provisions are for shareholders; reserves for suppliers", "Provisions cannot exceed $100"], 0, "Provisions (e.g. for depreciation, tax, bad debts) reduce net profit; reserves retain earned surpluses.", "Provisions and Reserves"),
            ("A Revenue Reserve is a reserve created out of trading profits that is:", ["Legally available for distribution to shareholders as dividends", "Restricted strictly to purchasing factory land", "Unavailable for dividends by statute", "Used only to redeem preference shares"], 0, "Revenue reserves (e.g. general reserve, retained earnings) may be distributed as dividends.", "Corporate Reserves"),
            ("Which capital reserve cannot be distributed as cash dividends under corporate statute?", ["Share Premium Account and Capital Redemption Reserve", "Retained Earnings", "General Reserve", "Profit and Loss Credit Balance"], 0, "Capital reserves protect creditors and cannot be paid out as ordinary cash dividends.", "Company Law"),
